You're right — floating elements can break the normal flow and cause them to collapse or shift in unexpected ways. However, if you float them right and give the container a defined width, they usually return to their normal position quickly, and the height issue is more about the parent container than the children.

A quick fix is to add a default property to the container:

```css
.navbar {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  height: 60px; /* or whatever height you want */
}
```

That `height` ensures the container holds its children vertically, and the `justify-content: space-between` pushes them apart. The links will then sit on the same row without messing with the page layout.

If you want to keep the `float: right` approach (and avoid the `height` hack), you could add a small margin to the container or use `margin-left: auto` on the `.nav-links`:

```css
.navbar {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  margin-left: auto; /* pushes links to the right */
}
```

Either way works — flexbox is much more predictable than float-based layout for this kind of thing.

